    given-names: Dong Hyun
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9883-0229"
    affiliation: "Inha University College of Medicine"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Raspberry-Kim/donghyun-lab13"
url: "https://raspberry-kim.github.io/donghyun-lab13/"
abstract: >-
  Donghyunram is a browser-based, noncommercial toy that follows only the
  skeleton of Rampage (Bally Midway, arcade, 1986; MS-DOS port published by
  Activision, 1988; Game Boy Advance compilation Paperboy / Rampage,
  Destination Software, 2005): a monster climbs buildings, punches windows
  until the building collapses, eats people and vehicles to regain stamina,
  and moves on through twelve cities. Unlike the original it is strictly
  single-player; the five monsters (Podong, Shushu, Lion, Tailcopter and
  Doomguy, all characters of the Donghyun series) differ in strength,
  toughness, reach, walking speed and fall speed, and have deliberately
  different body proportions. Stamina drains far more slowly and eating
  restores far more than in the original, so that long play near full
  stamina is the norm; the four eating animations are exaggerated. The
  only settings, the monster (1-5), the starting city (1-12) and the number
  of lives (1-9, 0 for unlimited), are chosen through a program written in
  the esoteric programming language Donghyun-lang. Input is keyboard,
  gamepad, mouse and touch (tap the monster to punch, tap a place to walk
  or climb there, flick up to jump), with fullscreen; the game is saved in
  three browser slots and can be exported to and imported from a .dhsave
  file. All sprites, cities and sounds are generated by code; no asset of
  the original games is used or extracted. The MIT license applies to the
  Donghyunram code and documentation; the page fonts are under the SIL
  Open Font License. See LICENSE and CREDITS.md.
keywords:
  - action game
  - Rampage
  - esoteric programming language
  - JavaScript
